intenance surface | Yes — Recommended |\n| **Option B** — Separate catalog per customer segment | + Native SFCC mechanism + No custom hook required | - Requires catalog duplication and ongoing sync - Significant merchandising overhead - Violates no-catalog-restructuring constraint | No |\n| **Option C** — Category-level visibility (assign VIC products to hidden category) | + Simple BM configuration | - Cannot gate by customer group natively - Products hidden from all users, not just non-VIC - Does not meet success criteria | No |\n\n### 3.2 Option Detail\n\n#### Option A — Custom attribute + SCAPI search hook + PDP guard (Recommended)\n\nA custom boolean product attribute `isVICExclusive` is added to the product object and made manageable via Business Manager. The attribute is indexed into the SFCC search index. A SCAPI search hook injects a refinement filter at query time to exclude VIC-exclusive products from results for non-VIC sessions. A separate controller-level guard handles direct PDP URL access. This satisfies all constraints — no catalog restructuring, works across all storefronts, and is fully merchandiser-controlled.\n\n#### Option B — Separate catalog per customer segment (Discarded)\n\nDiscarded — requires maintaining duplicate catalog structures per segment, violates the no-catalog-restructuring constraint, and introduces significant ongoing merchandising overhead.\n\n#### Option C — Category-level hidden category (Discarded)\n\nDiscarded — SFCC category visibility is binary (all users or no users) and cannot be gated by customer group. Does not meet the success criteria.\n\n---\n\n4. Recommended Option & Rationale\n---------------------------------\n\n### 4.1 Recommended Option\n\n**Option A — Custom attribute + SCAPI search hook + PDP guard**\n\n### 4.2 Rationale\n\nOption A is the only approach that satisfies all constraints without catalog restructuring and without introducing a dependency on platform capabilities SFCC does not natively offer. It enforces visibility at both the search layer (preventing discovery) and the PDP layer (preventing direct URL access), fully meeting the success criteria. Merchandiser control via Business Manager requires no developer involvement for day-to-day flagging.\n\n### 4.3 Trade-offs Accepted\n\nRe-indexing is required when the `isVICExclusive` attribute is added or changed on a product. For large catalogues, there may be a delay between a Merchandiser setting the flag in BM and the change being reflected in search results. This is accepted as a known SFCC platform behaviour and should be documented in the Merchandiser runbook.\n\n---\n\n5. Technical Implementation\n---------------------------\n\n### 5.1 High-Level Sequence\n\n1. Site Merchandiser sets `isVICExclusive = true` on the product in Business Manager.\n2. Standard SFCC re-indexing propagates the attribute value to the search index.\n3. Customer initiates a product search or browses a category on the storefront.\n4. SCAPI search hook intercepts the search request and evaluates the session customer group.\n5. If the customer is not in the VIC group, the hook injects a refinement filter: `isVICExclusive = false` (or absent). VIC-exclusive products are excluded from results.\n6. If the customer is in the VIC group, no filter is injected. All products including VIC-exclusive ones are returned.\n7. Customer navigates directly to a PDP URL for a VIC-exclusive product.\n8. Controller-level guard checks `isVICExclusive` on the product and the session customer group.\n9. If not VIC-eligible, return HTTP 404. If VIC-eligible, render PDP normally.\n\n### 5.2 Component / API Changes\n\n| File / Endpoint | Change Required | Type |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| BM Product Attribute Definition | Add `isVICExclusive` boolean attribute to the product system object. Mark as searchable/indexable. | New |\n| SCAPI Search Hook (`dw.ocapi.shop.product.search`) | Inject refinement filter on `isVICExclusive` based on session customer group memb